spec:
  grid: 12 rows x 12-13 cols, background 0
  sprites:
    count: 2-4 L-shaped sprites, each with one body color and one embedded marker pixel
    body: L-shape with horizontal leg 2-4 cells and vertical leg 2-4 cells sharing a bend corner; total body pixels = h_leg + v_leg - 1 (4-7)
    marker: single pixel of a different color, placed diagonally adjacent to the bend in the inner concavity of the L
    orientation: any of 4 rotations (bend at any bbox corner)
    all body colors distinct; marker color differs from its own body color
  controls:
    isolated single pixels on background; each matching a marker color of some sprite
    for each moved sprite, its matching control lies on the outer 45-degree diagonal ray from that sprite's current L-bend, opposite the embedded marker direction
    matched_controls: 1-2 (these sprites move); colors may also serve as body/marker in other sprites
  unmatched:
    0-2 sprites whose marker color has no matching control pixel stay in place
    0-1 control pixels whose color matches no marker (distractor) [observed]
  placement: all components (sprites + controls) are 4-disconnected from each other with >=2 Manhattan gap
  transformation:
    for each sprite whose marker color matches a control pixel, translate the entire sprite so the L-bend lands on that control pixel
    clear original sprite cells and consumed control; paste translated sprite on background
    translated sprites must stay in-bounds and not overlap any other component

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size ~12x12-13. Choose N=2-4 sprites and assign distinct body colors; assign marker colors and decide which K=1-2 sprites will move (their marker color gets a control pixel).
    2. For each sprite, generate an L-shape (random orientation, leg lengths 2-4) with marker in the inner diagonal of the bend.
    3. Place all sprites in their OUTPUT positions on the grid, ensuring no overlap, in-bounds, and >=2 gap between all components. Also place any unmatched control pixels (distractors).
    4. Derive input by reversing each moved sprite's translation: place its control pixel at the output bend location, then choose a valid original position whose L-bend is on the opposite 45-degree diagonal ray from that control (so the control is on the outer diagonal ray from the input bend), ensuring in-bounds, no overlap, and >=2 gap from all other input components; erase the sprite from its output position.
    5. Verify all solver assertions pass on the derived input.

